WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.233
Fine-tuning large language models normally requires
backpropagation,

00:00:05.313 --> 00:00:10.047
which stores activations and gradients and consumes
enormous memory.

00:00:10.127 --> 00:00:13.940
This paper, from Princeton University, introduces MeZO,

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:21.018
a memory-efficient zeroth-order optimizer that
fine-tunes language models using only forward passes.

00:00:21.098 --> 00:00:25.690
MeZO adapts the classical zeroth-order SGD method
to run in place,

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.017
so training costs the same memory as inference.

00:00:29.097 --> 00:00:34.538
On a single 80-gigabyte A100 GPU, MeZO can train a
30-billion-parameter model,

00:00:34.618 --> 00:00:38.785
where backpropagation with Adam fits only a
2.7-billion one.

00:00:38.865 --> 00:00:41.263
Across many tasks and model scales,

00:00:41.343 --> 00:00:46.430
MeZO matches fine-tuning performance with up to
twelve times less memory.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:50.831
Fine-tuning has driven much of the recent success
of language models,

00:00:50.911 --> 00:00:54.932
but it comes at a steep memory cost.

00:00:55.012 --> 00:00:59.033
Backpropagation must cache intermediate activations
and store gradients and optimizer states,

00:00:59.113 --> 00:01:03.134
which together can require up to twelve times the
memory of plain inference.

00:01:03.214 --> 00:01:07.236
As models scale into the tens of billions of parameters,

00:01:07.316 --> 00:01:11.337
this becomes the binding constraint.

00:01:11.417 --> 00:01:15.438
Concretely,

00:01:15.518 --> 00:01:19.539
a single eighty-gigabyte A100 GPU can run inference
on a thirty-billion-parameter model,

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:23.640
yet standard Adam fine-tuning on the same hardware
is limited to only a two-point-seven-billion-parameter model.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:30.913
Zeroth-order optimization offers a tempting escape:
it can estimate a gradient using only two forward passes,

00:01:30.993 --> 00:01:33.216
requiring no backpropagation at all.

00:01:33.296 --> 00:01:40.636
The catch is that classical analyses suggest
zeroth-order methods converge catastrophically slowly for large models,

00:01:40.716 --> 00:01:44.858
with the rate degrading in proportion to the number
of parameters.

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:49.912
That pessimism, combined with a naive
implementation that still doubles memory,

00:01:49.992 --> 00:01:54.773
is why zeroth-order methods have been overlooked
for modern language models.

00:01:54.853 --> 00:02:01.810
This paper asks whether that pessimism actually
holds when fine-tuning pre-trained models on downstream tasks.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:06.059
The paper makes four main contributions.

00:02:06.139 --> 00:02:10.008
First, it introduces MeZO,

00:02:10.088 --> 00:02:13.958
a memory-efficient zeroth-order optimizer that
adapts classical zeroth-order SGD to operate in place,

00:02:14.038 --> 00:02:17.907
so fine-tuning costs no more memory than inference.

00:02:17.987 --> 00:02:21.856
Second, through comprehensive experiments across
masked and autoregressive models,

00:02:21.936 --> 00:02:25.805
scales up to sixty-six billion parameters, and
classification, multiple-choice,

00:02:25.885 --> 00:02:29.755
and generation tasks,

00:02:29.835 --> 00:02:33.704
it shows MeZO matches full backpropagation
fine-tuning while using far less memory.

00:02:33.784 --> 00:02:37.653
Third,

00:02:37.733 --> 00:02:41.602
it demonstrates compatibility with
parameter-efficient methods like LoRA and prefix tuning,

00:02:41.682 --> 00:02:45.552
and the ability to optimize non-differentiable
objectives such as accuracy or F1.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:49.501
Fourth,

00:02:49.581 --> 00:02:53.450
it supplies theory explaining why MeZO converges
quickly despite the enormous parameter count.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:58.493
MeZO is built on a classical zeroth-order gradient
estimator called SPSA,

00:02:58.573 --> 00:03:02.001
or simultaneous perturbation stochastic approximation.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:05.964
At each step it samples a single random Gaussian
direction z,

00:03:06.044 --> 00:03:09.928
adds epsilon times z to every parameter and records
the loss,

00:03:10.008 --> 00:03:13.306
then subtracts to get the loss on the opposite side.

00:03:13.386 --> 00:03:20.128
The difference of these two losses divided by twice
epsilon gives a scalar called the projected gradient,

00:03:20.208 --> 00:03:26.495
and the parameters are then updated by moving along
z scaled by this scalar and the learning rate.

00:03:26.575 --> 00:03:31.952
The crucial engineering insight is that instead of
storing the full random vector z,

00:03:32.032 --> 00:03:33.642
which would double memory,

00:03:33.722 --> 00:03:39.619
MeZO stores only the random seed and regenerates z
deterministically each time it is needed.

00:03:39.699 --> 00:03:44.362
This lets the entire perturb, evaluate, and update
cycle happen in place,

00:03:44.442 --> 00:03:47.870
so training uses exactly the same memory as inference.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.673
The evaluation is deliberately broad.

00:03:50.753 --> 00:03:52.973
On the masked-language-model side,

00:03:53.053 --> 00:03:57.912
it uses RoBERTa-large on six
sentence-classification and inference tasks,

00:03:57.992 --> 00:04:05.828
tested in both a few-shot regime with sixteen
examples per class and a many-shot regime with five hundred and twelve.

00:04:05.908 --> 00:04:07.654
On the autoregressive side,

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:13.811
it uses OPT models ranging from one-point-three
billion up to sixty-six billion parameters,

00:04:13.891 --> 00:04:16.788
evaluated on SuperGLUE classification tasks,

00:04:16.868 --> 00:04:19.697
multiple-choice tasks like COPA and ReCoRD,

00:04:19.777 --> 00:04:22.809
and generation tasks including SQuAD and DROP.

00:04:22.889 --> 00:04:27.950
Baselines include zero-shot prediction, in-context
learning, linear probing,

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:29.980
and standard Adam fine-tuning.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:37.771
The headline result is that MeZO closes most of the
gap to backpropagation fine-tuning at a fraction of the memory.

00:04:37.851 --> 00:04:39.334
On OPT-thirteen-billion,

00:04:39.414 --> 00:04:44.936
MeZO comes within about one percent of full Adam
fine-tuning on seven of eleven tasks,

00:04:45.016 --> 00:04:47.997
while consuming only one-twelfth of the memory,

00:04:48.077 --> 00:04:53.403
and it clearly beats zero-shot prediction,
in-context learning, and linear probing.

00:04:53.483 --> 00:04:56.139
On RoBERTa-large in the many-shot setting,

00:04:56.219 --> 00:04:59.526
it lands within roughly five percent of fine-tuning.

00:04:59.606 --> 00:05:02.587
Beyond memory, MeZO is also faster in practice,

00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:08.580
requiring about half the GPU-hours of Adam
fine-tuning for a thirty-billion-parameter model.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.923
Several ablations probe MeZO's flexibility.

00:05:12.003 --> 00:05:17.515
Combining MeZO with parameter-efficient methods,
namely LoRA and prefix tuning,

00:05:17.595 --> 00:05:20.983
gives accuracy on par with tuning all parameters,

00:05:21.063 --> 00:05:23.814
showing the two approaches compose well.

00:05:23.894 --> 00:05:29.122
Using a single random perturbation per step, rather
than averaging several,

00:05:29.202 --> 00:05:34.785
turns out to be the most efficient setting for a
fixed number of forward passes.

00:05:34.865 --> 00:05:39.244
Because MeZO only needs loss values and never
actual gradients,

00:05:39.324 --> 00:05:43.136
it can optimize non-differentiable objectives directly,

00:05:43.216 --> 00:05:45.968
such as maximizing accuracy or F1 score.

00:05:46.048 --> 00:05:49.436
And against a prior zeroth-order baseline, BBTv2,

00:05:49.516 --> 00:05:53.470
MeZO improves accuracy by up to eleven percentage points.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.955
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:05:56.035 --> 00:06:03.108
MeZO delivers up to a twelvefold reduction in
memory compared with Adam fine-tuning on OPT-thirteen-billion,

00:06:03.188 --> 00:06:05.492
using no more memory than inference.

00:06:05.572 --> 00:06:07.677
On a single eighty-gigabyte A100,

00:06:07.757 --> 00:06:14.234
it trains a thirty-billion-parameter model where
backpropagation fits only two-point-seven billion.

00:06:14.314 --> 00:06:19.068
It matches fine-tuning within about one percent on
seven of eleven tasks,

00:06:19.148 --> 00:06:23.770
and it roughly halves the GPU-hours needed at the
thirty-billion scale.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:29.258
The lasting takeaway is that fine-tuning no longer
strictly requires backpropagation.

00:06:29.338 --> 00:06:34.019
With MeZO, you can adapt very large language models
using only forward passes,

00:06:34.099 --> 00:06:35.972
at the memory cost of inference,

00:06:36.052 --> 00:06:40.366
and still match the quality of gradient-based
fine-tuning on many tasks.

00:06:40.446 --> 00:06:45.737
This overturns the classical worry that
zeroth-order methods must scale badly with model

00:06:45.817 --> 00:06:48.972
size: when you start from a strong pre-trained model,

00:06:49.052 --> 00:06:54.160
convergence is governed by the effective local
structure of the loss landscape rather

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.357
than the sheer number of parameters,

00:06:56.437 --> 00:06:59.470
which is why MeZO works at billion-parameter scale.
